The routine tan-s computes the tangent of a whole number of degrees, by its partial fractions.

Result: 100 tan x rounded to nearest, for x a whole number of degrees from 0 to 359, where it fits a cell: x
mod 180 from 0 to 84 and from 96 to 179 (-951..951). Near the poles it saturates: 999 for x mod 180 from 85
to 90, -999 from 91 to 95.

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 13. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 13-98 (86 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 77-85, 97-98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 21113 instructions a call, 15902.7 on average over the domain, counted from its first cell to its
return jump. Worst error: 1.362 hundredths from the exact value, at x = 54; 124 of the 360 inputs are not the
correctly rounded value.
Address bases: cell 89 holds ADD 85; cell 90 holds SUB 85. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from
every address in its instructions that points into cells 13-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99
stay as they are.

